window.docContents[141].resolve({"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___4___-Filtrations/Section-3___4___-Filtrations-___-heading-and-setup/#Chapter3___Introduction_to_3___4___heading-1":{"contents":"Let A be an algebra. Let V be a representation of A.\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t3. General results of representation theory\t3.4. Filtrations\tSection 3.4: Filtrations — heading and setup","header":"3.4. Filtrations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___4___-Filtrations/Section-3___4___-Filtrations-___-heading-and-setup/#Chapter3___Introduction_to_3___4___heading-1"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___13___-Historical-interlude___-William-Rowan-Hamilton___s-quaternion-of-geometry___-algebra___-metaphysics___-and-poetry/#chapter-04___section-4-13":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t4. Representations of finite groups: Basic results","header":"4.13. Historical interlude: William Rowan Hamilton's quaternion of geometry, algebra, metaphysics, and poetry","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___13___-Historical-interlude___-William-Rowan-Hamilton___s-quaternion-of-geometry___-algebra___-metaphysics___-and-poetry/#chapter-04___section-4-13"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___5___-Gabriel___s-theorem/Preamble-to-Gabriel___s-theorem/#Chapter6___Discussion_before_Theorem6___5___2":{"contents":"We are now able to formulate Gabriel's theorem using roots.\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t6. Quiver representations\t6.5. Gabriel's theorem\tPreamble to Gabriel's theorem","header":"Preamble to Gabriel's theorem","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___5___-Gabriel___s-theorem/Preamble-to-Gabriel___s-theorem/#Chapter6___Discussion_before_Theorem6___5___2"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___10___-Historical-interlude___-Eilenberg___-Mac-Lane___-and-___general-abstract-nonsense___/Section-7___10___-Historical-interlude___-Eilenberg___-Mac-Lane___-and-general-abstract-nonsense/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___10___heading-1":{"contents":"","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories\t7.10. Historical interlude: Eilenberg, Mac Lane, and \"general abstract nonsense\"\tSection 7.10: Historical interlude: Eilenberg, Mac Lane, and general abstract nonsense","header":"7.10. Historical interlude: Eilenberg, Mac Lane, and \"general abstract nonsense\"","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___10___-Historical-interlude___-Eilenberg___-Mac-Lane___-and-___general-abstract-nonsense___/Section-7___10___-Historical-interlude___-Eilenberg___-Mac-Lane___-and-general-abstract-nonsense/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___10___heading-1"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Section-8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/#Chapter8___Introduction_8___1":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t8. Homological algebra\t8.1. Projective and injective modules\tSection 8.1: Projective and injective modules","header":"Section 8.1: Projective and injective modules","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Section-8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/#Chapter8___Introduction_8___1"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents/Lifting-of-idempotents-from-quotient-by-nilpotent-ideal/#Chapter9___Proposition9___1___1":{"contents":"Proposition 9.1.1. Let e_0 \\in A/I be an idempotent, i.e., e_0^2 = e_0. There exists an idempotent e \\in A which is a lift of e_0 (i.e., it projects to e_0 under the reduction modulo I). This idempotent is unique up to conjugation by an element of 1 + I.\n\nProof. Let us first establish the statement in the case when I^2 = 0. Note that in this case I is a left and right module over A/I. Let e_* be any lift of e_0 to A. Then e_*^2 - e_* = a \\in I, and e_0 a = a e_0. We look for e in the form e = e_* + b, b \\in I. The equation for b is\n\n\ne_0 b + b e_0 - b = -a.\n\n\nSet b = (1 - 2e_0)a. Then\n\n\ne_0 b + b e_0 - b = -2e_0 a - (1 - 2e_0)a = -a,\n\nso e is an idempotent. To classify other solutions, set e' = e + c. For e' to be an idempotent, we must have ec + ce - c = 0. This is equivalent to saying that ece = 0 and (1 - e)c(1 - e) = 0, so c = ec(1 - e) + (1 - e)ce = [e, [e, c]]. Hence e' = (1 + [c, e])e(1 + [c, e])^{-1}.\n\nNow, in the general case, we prove by induction in k that there exists a lift e_k of e_{k-1} to A/I^{k+1}, and it is unique up to conjugation by an element of 1 + I^k (this is sufficient as I is nilpotent). Assume it is true for k = m - 1, and let us prove it for k = m. So we have an idempotent e_{m-1} \\in A/I^m, and we have to lift it to A/I^{m+1}. But (I^m)^2 = 0 in A/I^{m+1}, so we are done. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t9. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\t9.1. Lifting of idempotents\tLifting of idempotents from quotient by nilpotent ideal","header":"Lifting of idempotents from quotient by nilpotent ideal","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents/Lifting-of-idempotents-from-quotient-by-nilpotent-ideal/#Chapter9___Proposition9___1___1"}});
